{"id":"windows-intro","corpus":"k8s","title":"Windows containers in Kubernetes","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/windows/intro/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Windows containers in Kubernetes"},{"kind":"p","text":"Windows applications constitute a large portion of the services and applications that run in many organizations. Windows containers provide a way to encapsulate processes and package dependencies, making it easier to use DevOps practices and follow cloud native patterns for Windows applications."},{"kind":"p","text":"Organizations with investments in Windows-based applications and Linux-based applications don't have to look for separate orchestrators to manage their workloads, leading to increased operational efficiencies across their deployments, regardless of operating system."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Windows nodes in Kubernetes"},{"kind":"p","text":"To enable the orchestration of Windows containers in Kubernetes, include Windows nodes in your existing Linux cluster. Scheduling Windows containers in Pods on Kubernetes is similar to scheduling Linux-based containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to run Windows containers, your Kubernetes cluster must include multiple operating systems. While you can only run the control plane on Linux, you can deploy worker nodes running either Windows or Linux."},{"kind":"p","text":"Windows nodes are supported provided that the operating system is Windows Server 2022 or Windows Server 2025."},{"kind":"p","text":"This document uses the term Windows containers to mean Windows containers with process isolation. Kubernetes does not support running Windows containers with Hyper-V isolation."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Compatibility and limitations"},{"kind":"p","text":"Some node features are only available if you use a specific container runtime; others are not available on Windows nodes, including:"},{"kind":"li","text":"HugePages: not supported for Windows containers"},{"kind":"li","text":"Privileged containers: not supported for Windows containers. HostProcess Containers offer similar functionality."},{"kind":"li","text":"TerminationGracePeriod: requires containerD"},{"kind":"p","text":"Not all features of shared namespaces are supported. See API compatibility for more details."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Windows OS version compatibility for details on the Windows versions that Kubernetes is tested against."},{"kind":"p","text":"From an API and kubectl perspective, Windows containers behave in much the same way as Linux-based containers. However, there are some notable differences in key functionality which are outlined in this section."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Comparison with Linux"},{"kind":"p","text":"Key Kubernetes elements work the same way in Windows as they do in Linux. This section refers to several key workload abstractions and how they map to Windows."},{"kind":"li","text":"Pods A Pod is the basic building block of Kubernetes–the smallest and simplest unit in the Kubernetes object model that you create or deploy. You may not deploy Windows and Linux containers in the same Pod. All containers in a Pod are scheduled onto a single Node where each Node represents a specific platform and architecture. The following Pod capabilities, properties and events are supported with Windows containers:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Single or multiple containers per Pod with process isolation and volume sharing"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod status fields"},{"kind":"li","text":"Readiness, liveness, and startup probes"},{"kind":"li","text":"postStart & preStop container lifecycle hooks"},{"kind":"li","text":"ConfigMap, Secrets: as environment variables or volumes"},{"kind":"li","text":"emptyDir volumes"},{"kind":"li","text":"Named pipe host mounts"},{"kind":"li","text":"Resource limits"},{"kind":"li","text":"OS field: The .spec.os.name field should be set to windows to indicate that the current Pod uses Windows containers. If you set the .spec.os.name field to windows, you must not set the following fields in the .spec of that Pod: In the above list, wildcards (*) indicate all elements in a list. For example, spec.containers[*].securityContext refers to the SecurityContext object for all containers. If any of these fields is specified, the Pod will not be admitted by the API server."},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.hostPID"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.hostIPC"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.seLinuxOptions"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.seccompProfile"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.fsGroup"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.fsGroupChangePolicy"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.sysctls"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.shareProcessNamespace"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.runAsUser"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.runAsGroup"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.securityContext.supplementalGroups"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.seLinuxOptions"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.seccompProfile"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.capabilities"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.privileged"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.procMount"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.runAsUser"},{"kind":"li","text":"spec.containers[*].securityContext.runAsGroup"},{"kind":"li","text":"Workload resources including:"},{"kind":"li","text":"ReplicaSet"},{"kind":"li","text":"Deployment"},{"kind":"li","text":"StatefulSet"},{"kind":"li","text":"DaemonSet"},{"kind":"li","text":"Job"},{"kind":"li","text":"CronJob"},{"kind":"li","text":"ReplicationController"},{"kind":"li","text":"Services See Load balancing and Services for more details."},{"kind":"p","text":"Pods, workload resources, and Services are critical elements to managing Windows workloads on Kubernetes. However, on their own they are not enough to enable the proper lifecycle management of Windows workloads in a dynamic cloud native environment."},{"kind":"li","text":"kubectl exec"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod and container metrics"},{"kind":"li","text":"Horizontal pod autoscaling"},{"kind":"li","text":"Resource quotas"},{"kind":"li","text":"Scheduler preemption"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Command line options for the kubelet"},{"kind":"p","text":"Some kubelet command line options behave differently on Windows, as described below:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The --windows-priorityclass lets you set the scheduling priority of the kubelet process (see CPU resource management)"},{"kind":"li","text":"The --kube-reserved, --system-reserved , and --eviction-hard flags update NodeAllocatable"}],"lemmas":{"windows":["window","windows"],"containers":["container","containers"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"applications":["application","applications"],"constitutes":["constitutes","constitute"],"constituted":["constituted","constitute"],"constituting":["constituting","constitute"],"larges":["large","larges"],"larger":["larger","large"],"largest":["largest","large"],"portions":["portion","portions"],"services":["service","services"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"organizations":["organization","organizations"],"provides":["provides","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"encapsulates":["encapsulates","encapsulate"],"processes":["process","processes"],"processing":["processing","process"],"processed":["processed","process"],"packages":["package","packages"],"packaging":["packaging","package"],"packaged":["packaged","package"],"dependencies":["dependency","dependencies"],"making":["making","make"],"makings":["making","mak